#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
vaultsync — rotating ZFS backups to removable USB drives.

Runs on the ZFS host (e.g. Proxmox). Standard library only.

  vaultsync status              overview: source pool, ports, attached drives, jobs
  vaultsync ports               list physical USB ports and what is plugged in
  vaultsync drives              list attached USB disks
  vaultsync init /dev/sdX --label NAME     ERASE a disk and make it a backup drive
  vaultsync plan  [POOL|LABEL]  show what a sync would do (full/incremental, size)
  vaultsync sync  [POOL|LABEL]  back up the source pool to the drive, prints COMPLETE
  vaultsync verify [POOL|LABEL] scrub the backup drive and report errors
  vaultsync eject [POOL|LABEL]  export the drive pool and detach the disk
  vaultsync jobs                list recent jobs
  vaultsync log JOB_ID          show a job log
  vaultsync cancel JOB_ID       stop a running job
  vaultsync reconnect [POOL]    reattach a drive that was unplugged while in use
  vaultsync forget POOL         delete the host's bookmarks and job history for a retired drive
  vaultsync cleanup             remove stale vaultsync snapshots from the source
  vaultsync agent               run the HTTP agent used by the dashboard

How it works
  * Each backup drive is a single-disk ZFS pool named vs-<id>.
  * Each sync snapshots the source recursively and sends every dataset to
    <pool>/data/... . After success the source keeps only a ZFS *bookmark*
    per dataset per drive, so a drive that is away for months pins no space.
  * Receives are resumable (zfs recv -s): an interrupted sync continues where
    it stopped the next time that drive is synced.
  * The drive keeps only the most recent snapshot.
"""

